### How does TreeSet sort custom objects?

TreeSet wraps a TreeMap internally (elements are keys, values are a dummy PRESENT constant). All sorting behavior comes from the TreeMap's Red-Black tree.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class TreeSetSortingDemo {
    // Custom class implementing Comparable
    record Account(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance)
        implements Comparable<Account> {
        @Override
        public int compareTo(Account other) {
            int cmp = this.balance.compareTo(other.balance);
            return cmp != 0 ? cmp : this.id.compareTo(other.id); // tiebreaker!
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Natural ordering (Comparable)
        TreeSet<Account> byBalance = new TreeSet<>();
        byBalance.add(new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("150000")));
        byBalance.add(new Account("CUR3001", "Priya", new BigDecimal("75000")));
        byBalance.add(new Account("FD4001", "Amit", new BigDecimal("500000")));

        System.out.println("Natural order (by balance):");
        byBalance.forEach(a -> System.out.println("  " + a.id() + ": " + a.balance()));

        // Custom Comparator: sort by holder name
        TreeSet<Account> byName = new TreeSet<>(
            Comparator.comparing(Account::holder).thenComparing(Account::id)
        );
        byName.addAll(byBalance);
        System.out.println("\nBy holder name:");
        byName.forEach(a -> System.out.println("  " + a.holder() + ": " + a.id()));
    }
}
```

```text
Natural order (by balance):
  CUR3001: 75000
  SAV2001: 150000
  FD4001: 500000

By holder name:
  Amit: FD4001
  Priya: CUR3001
  Rajesh: SAV2001
```

### Why is the tiebreaker important?

If two accounts have the same balance and no tiebreaker, `compareTo()` returns 0, and TreeSet treats them as duplicates. The second account is silently dropped.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class TiebreakerDemo {
    record AccountNoTie(String id, BigDecimal balance)
        implements Comparable<AccountNoTie> {
        @Override
        public int compareTo(AccountNoTie other) {
            return this.balance.compareTo(other.balance); // no tiebreaker!
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeSet<AccountNoTie> set = new TreeSet<>();
        set.add(new AccountNoTie("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("150000")));
        set.add(new AccountNoTie("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("150000"))); // same balance!
        System.out.println("Size (no tiebreaker): " + set.size()); // 1! CUR3001 dropped!
    }
}
```

```text
Size (no tiebreaker): 1
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Using TreeSet for a class without Comparable

```java
class Transaction { String id; } // no Comparable!
TreeSet<Transaction> set = new TreeSet<>();
set.add(new Transaction()); // first add OK (nothing to compare)
set.add(new Transaction()); // ClassCastException!

// Fix: provide Comparator
TreeSet<Transaction> fixed = new TreeSet<>(Comparator.comparing(t -> t.id));
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **NavigableSet methods.** TreeSet provides first(), last(), lower(e), floor(e), ceiling(e), higher(e), subSet(), headSet(), tailSet(), descendingSet(), pollFirst(), pollLast().

- **ConcurrentSkipListSet.** The thread-safe equivalent of TreeSet. Uses a skip list instead of a Red-Black tree.

- **TreeSet.add returns false for compareTo-0 duplicates.** The return value tells you whether the element was actually added.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What happens if two elements have the same compareTo result?" – TreeSet treats them as duplicates.
- "How do you prevent accidental duplicate dropping?" – Add a tiebreaker to the comparator.
- "Can TreeSet contain null?" – No (compareTo throws NPE).
- "What is the time complexity of TreeSet.add?" – O(log n).
